Author:     Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
AuthorDate: Tue Aug 11 11:11:58 2026 -0500
Commit:     Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Tue Aug 11 11:11:58 2026 -0500

    fix(engine): S3 review round 2 — per-handle count cache, rebuild vs FTS
    
    Two confirmed findings on the JSONL-canonical write path.
    
    The out-of-band count check bricked any store opened by two handles at
    once: each handle carried its own `_facts_indexed` counter, seeded at
    open, so the second committer stamped a stale total over a correct one
    and the next open refused a perfectly consistent store, naming writers
    that never ran. The cached counters are gone. Every stamp now derives
    its counts from the *committed* marker, read inside the write
    transaction after the INSERT has taken sqlite's write lock — a
    concurrent writer's stamp is already visible and nothing further can
    land before the commit. Catch-up passes an explicit committed baseline
    down to `_index_lines`/`_tail_forward`, and a pre-marker store adopts
    its current row counts as the baseline instead of guessing zero.
    
    `_rebuild`'s `DELETE FROM facts` resets sqlite's rowid counter (no
    AUTOINCREMENT), so re-indexed rows take rowids that previously named
    other facts. `facts_fts.fact_rowid` keys on exactly that rowid and
    `fts_state.last_rowid` is the incremental watermark: a surviving FTS
    index resolved stale text to new facts and never indexed them. Both
    tables are dropped in the rebuild transaction, leaving search in its
    honest `missing` / run-reindex state.
    
    Three regressions, each verified to fail against the prior code.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

