Author: Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Aug 11 10:36:17 2026 -0500

    feat(store): S2 — JSONL export + rebuild migration oracle
    
    Export an existing sqlite store to the canonical interleaved JSONL log in
    GLOBAL RECEIPT ORDER, and rebuild a fresh sqlite index back from it. The
    bridge for stores that predate the authority flip
    (design/architecture/jsonl-canonical-store).
    
    Ordering rule: the tick row carries its own join — fact_cursor names the
    newest fact by witness order (rowid) at mint time, and window_hash commits
    to (window_start, fact_cursor] by rowid. So a tick's receipt position is
    "immediately after the fact at its cursor"; ties (a tick minted with no new
    facts) break on tick append order, an empty cursor sorts ahead of every
    fact, and an unresolvable cursor (pre-chain NULL, or a fact absent from this
    custody context) inherits the nearest preceding resolvable anchor.
    
    Rebuild takes its schema from engine.SqliteStore's own creation path and
    inserts rows verbatim in log order — an index rebuild, not a re-emit, so no
    mint machinery runs and no hash or signature can change.
    
    Era-aware on schema WIDTH, not just NULLs: a truly pre-chain store has no
    chain and no signature columns, so column presence is read from the schema
    and absent columns select as NULL — an absent column and a NULL value are
    the same era claim.
    
    CLI: loops store export <vertex> [target] [--rebuild PATH] [--json].
    
    Oracle test: fixture stores across all three eras (pre-chain narrow schema,
    chained, signed — plus the mixed uuid4/ULID id eras and a mixed per-observer
    fact-signing era) round-trip to byte-identical rows, byte-identical
    tick_row_hash values, identical chain head, and passing verify_chain/
    verify_facts on both sides.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

