## Findings

1. **Blocker — a successful append can stamp past an unrecovered durable line.**  
   [jsonl_store.py:346](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/jsonl_store.py:346) rolls back SQLite after any post-fsync failure but leaves the durable line in JSONL. A subsequent append on the same or another already-open handle does not reconcile the log first; [jsonl_store.py:370](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/jsonl_store.py:370) appends another line and stamps the full file size. Fault injection produced three log IDs but only the first and third in SQLite; every later open reported `synced`. This also occurs if writer A crashes after fsync while a long-lived writer B is waiting.

2. **Major — existing indexes never catch up on read resolution.**  
   [jsonl_store.py:214](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/jsonl_store.py:214) returns immediately whenever the `.db` exists. Consequently [resolved_index](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/jsonl_store.py:226) materializes fresh clones but never tails an existing index after the documented crash window. In scratch, adding a durable second line and calling `resolved_index()` left SQLite at one row. Read-only invocations can omit canonical facts indefinitely until a writer constructs `JsonlStore`.

3. **Major — direct SQLite updates outside the final row are not detected.**  
   [jsonl_store.py:512](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/jsonl_store.py:512) checks only the last consumed line, while [jsonl_store.py:437](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/jsonl_store.py:437) checks only counts. With two facts, directly changing the first payload preserved both checks; reopen returned `synced` and exposed the tampered value. Thus “out-of-band direct sqlite writes are detected on next open” holds for inserts and last-row updates, not arbitrary writes.

